1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,394
Welcome to lesson four, the start of level one.

2
00:00:03,494 --> 00:00:10,768
Today you'll get a working Kubernetes cluster, look at what's inside it, and learn the kubectl commands you'll use every day.

3
00:00:12,118 --> 00:00:14,224
There are many ways to get a cluster.

4
00:00:14,324 --> 00:00:22,478
On a laptop, kind and minikube are popular. In the cloud, there are managed services like EKS, GKE and AKS.

5
00:00:22,578 --> 00:00:30,892
For learning, start with the free Killercoda Kubernetes playground. It gives you a real cluster with a control-plane node and a worker node, right in your browser.

6
00:00:32,242 --> 00:00:38,300
kubectl is only a client. It sends HTTPS requests to the cluster's API server.

7
00:00:38,400 --> 00:00:48,970
It reads its settings from a file called kubeconfig. That file lists clusters, users, and contexts. A context bundles a cluster, a user and a default namespace.

8
00:00:49,070 --> 00:00:57,864
Always check which context you're using before running a destructive command. Many outages started with someone working on production while thinking they were on dev.

9
00:00:59,214 --> 00:01:03,848
Let's look around. kubectl get nodes shows the machines in the cluster.

10
00:01:03,948 --> 00:01:10,422
kubectl get pods in the kube-system namespace shows Kubernetes' own components, which also run as Pods.

11
00:01:10,522 --> 00:01:26,132
You'll recognize etcd, the database. The API server, the front door. The scheduler, which places Pods. And the controller manager, which runs the reconciliation loops. There's also CoreDNS, kube-proxy, and a network plugin.

12
00:01:27,482 --> 00:01:32,308
Namespaces divide a cluster into separate areas. Every cluster starts with a few.

13
00:01:32,408 --> 00:01:39,746
default is where your objects go if you don't choose a namespace. kube-system holds Kubernetes' own components, so don't put your apps there.

14
00:01:39,846 --> 00:01:47,360
kubectl api-resources lists every object type, with handy short names like po for pods and svc for services.

15
00:01:48,710 --> 00:01:51,024
There are two ways to create objects.

16
00:01:51,124 --> 00:01:56,814
Imperative commands like kubectl run are quick, which is great for experiments and exams.

17
00:01:56,914 --> 00:02:05,948
Declarative means you write a YAML file and run kubectl apply. The file is a record you can review and keep in Git. Use this for anything real.

18
00:02:07,298 --> 00:02:13,708
Here's a shortcut. Add dash dash dry run equals client and dash o yaml to almost any create command.

19
00:02:13,808 --> 00:02:19,562
kubectl prints the YAML instead of creating the object. Save it to a file, edit it, and apply it.

20
00:02:20,912 --> 00:02:23,194
Now the commands you'll use every day.

21
00:02:23,294 --> 00:02:35,016
kubectl get lists objects. Dash A means all namespaces, dash o wide adds the IP and node, and dash l filters by label. Dash o yaml shows the full object, including its status.

22
00:02:35,116 --> 00:02:43,702
kubectl describe shows details, and at the bottom, the Events. That's the Pod's life story, and usually where you'll find what went wrong.

23
00:02:45,052 --> 00:02:52,950
kubectl logs shows a container's output. Dash f follows it, and dash dash previous shows the logs from before the last crash.

24
00:02:53,050 --> 00:03:00,340
kubectl exec runs a command inside the container. The double dash separates kubectl's options from the command.

25
00:03:00,440 --> 00:03:08,098
port-forward lets you reach a Pod from your own terminal, delete removes objects, and explain is built-in documentation for every field.

26
00:03:09,448 --> 00:03:14,226
Here's an important lesson. Create a Pod with kubectl run, then delete it.

27
00:03:14,326 --> 00:03:21,744
It's gone for good. Nothing recreates it, because a plain Pod has no manager. If its node dies, the Pod is lost too.

28
00:03:21,844 --> 00:03:28,862
That's why you almost never create Pods directly. You create a Deployment, which keeps them running. That's the next lesson.

29
00:03:30,212 --> 00:03:33,086
In the lab, you'll break things on purpose.

30
00:03:33,186 --> 00:03:41,788
A Pod with a tag that doesn't exist shows ErrImagePull, then ImagePullBackOff. Describe shows the reason: the image wasn't found.

31
00:03:41,888 --> 00:03:50,570
A container that exits with an error shows CrashLoopBackOff, and its restart count keeps climbing. kubectl logs with dash dash previous shows why.

32
00:03:51,920 --> 00:03:53,114
Let's recap.

33
00:03:53,214 --> 00:04:00,136
kubectl talks to the API server using contexts from your kubeconfig, so always check which cluster you're on.

34
00:04:00,236 --> 00:04:04,278
Write YAML and apply it, and generate starting YAML with dry run.

35
00:04:04,378 --> 00:04:12,868
Get, describe, logs, exec, port-forward, delete and explain are your daily tools. And describe's Events are where errors hide.

36
00:04:12,968 --> 00:04:19,714
Finally, bare Pods aren't recreated. Next lesson, you'll use Deployments to run apps that heal themselves.
